WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.367
Neural network basics.

2
00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:04.762
Why does stacking layers help at all?

3
00:00:04.762 --> 00:00:07.367
The answer is one small function between them.

4
00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:11.867
A neural network is layers of perceptron-like units.

5
00:00:12.167 --> 00:00:17.200
But the hard threshold is replaced by a smooth activation, like ReLU or tanh.

6
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:19.622
That activation is not decoration.

7
00:00:19.622 --> 00:00:20.933
It's the whole point.

8
00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:23.767
Look at what happens without one.

9
00:00:24.067 --> 00:00:26.067
The first layer applies a linear map.

10
00:00:26.367 --> 00:00:28.200
The second layer applies another.

11
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.678
But two linear maps multiply into a single matrix.

12
00:00:31.678 --> 00:00:33.967
A hundred layers would still be one.

13
00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:36.700
So the recipe alternates.

14
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:37.900
A linear step.

15
00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.567
Then a non-linear bend.

16
00:00:39.867 --> 00:00:42.077
Then linear again, and bend again.

17
00:00:42.077 --> 00:00:45.067
Now the network can fold and warp input space.

18
00:00:46.267 --> 00:00:47.933
Here are two common choices.

19
00:00:48.233 --> 00:00:52.546
ReLU just returns zero for negatives and passes positives through.

20
00:00:52.546 --> 00:00:54.833
It's cheap, and the modern default.

21
00:00:55.133 --> 00:00:58.833
Tanh squashes everything smoothly between minus one and one.

22
00:01:00.033 --> 00:01:03.367
Now the perceptron's nemesis, XOR, in PyTorch.

23
00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:06.139
Two linear layers with a tanh between them.

24
00:01:06.139 --> 00:01:08.267
Delete the tanh, and it never learns.

25
00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:10.233
We train with the usual loop.

26
00:01:10.533 --> 00:01:12.800
And the network gets all four answers right.

27
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.667
Here's the real-world payoff.

28
00:01:15.967 --> 00:01:20.467
In exoplanet vetting, the label depends on how period and depth interact.

29
00:01:20.767 --> 00:01:25.300
Logistic regression, one linear boundary, tops out at seventy one percent.

30
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:31.867
One hidden layer with ReLU jumps to eighty eight, because its units learn the folds where the interaction lives.

31
00:01:33.067 --> 00:01:33.797
Let's recap.

32
00:01:33.797 --> 00:01:36.233
Stacked linear layers collapse into one.

33
00:01:36.533 --> 00:01:39.467
Activations break that collapse by bending the space.

34
00:01:39.767 --> 00:01:44.067
With enough units, one hidden layer can approximate any continuous function.

35
00:01:44.367 --> 00:01:47.556
Width gives capacity, depth gives composition.

36
00:01:47.556 --> 00:01:49.567
Next up: forward propagation.
